{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1790505601000-architect-apply-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md collab/1790505600000-architect-lease-sketch-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc | sed -n '1,570p'\"","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-27 13:19:51 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-27 20:19:51 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**Ruling: A, with a strict network boundary.** Keep `dao.stream.apply.cljc` and the VM FFI bridge unchanged. The VM constructs requests and validates responses through that namespace, and its walker appends those requests to the VM call stream ([ast_walker.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:195), [ffi.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:95), [ffi.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:225)). Moving this API into `dao.stream.rpc` would make core VM evaluation depend on a REPL client module. The plan retires `dao.stream.apply` **as a wire envelope** while expressly retaining the VM FFI bridge ([implementation plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:199)). The implementer’s dependency finding is correct ([findings](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790497441896-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice5.r4.claude.findings.md:8)).\n\nGive the implementer these slice 5 instructions:\n\n1. **Delete** `dao.stream.serving.cljc` and `dao.stream.rpc.ws.cljc`, and remove their REPL requires and call sites. The plan explicitly retires both ([implementation plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:188), [implementation plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:197)). Leave `dao.stream.apply.cljc`, `yin.vm.ffi.cljc`, and `yin.vm.ast_walker.cljc` intact.\n\n2. **Rework `dao.stream.rpc.cljc` for the REPL reflection pair.** Keep its caller-owned ID allocation, pending request, completion, and rebind state. Its current request encoder, response validator, and server alias still use the apply envelope ([rpc.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:100), [rpc.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:169), [rpc.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:295)). Replace those **network-path** uses with the REPL service’s request and answer values and direct `stream/append!`/`stream/next`; correlate by the RPC ID. Do not put `apply/request` or `apply/success-response` on either reflection. Translate reflection read failures into the existing client terminal vocabulary: `:dao.stream/transport-error` with `:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found` → `:dao.stream.apply/not-found`; with `:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone` → `:dao.stream.apply/detached`; bare `:dao.stream/end` → `:dao.stream.apply/ended`; other transport errors → `:dao.stream.apply/transport-error`. Preserve the underlying read result for diagnostics. The reflection returns those reasoned errors and `end` directly ([remote.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:603)); the RPC client and driver already distinguish a reconnectable `detached` terminal ([rpc.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:366), [driver.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:209)). **Translation alone is insufficient** while RPC still emits apply envelopes.\n\n3. **Rework `yin.repl.serve.cljc`, `connect.cljc`, and `adapter.cljc` together.** Serve one shared REPL state over two identities, `yin.repl/requests` and `yin.repl/answers`; retain the existing eval and incomplete-input decisions, but express replies as service answer values instead of apply success/error envelopes ([serve.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:559), [adapter.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:167)). The server composes the two retained streams with `dao.stream.remote`’s mirror over a WebSocket channel accepted through `dao.stream.ws-project/make-acceptor` and `accept-step!`. The client uses `dao.stream.ws-project/dial` and `dial-attach!`, then attaches both remote descriptors through `dao.stream.remote/attacher`; the requests reflection writes and the answers reflection reads ([ws_project.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:161), [ws_project.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:305), [ws_project.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:348), [remote.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:734)). Keep the URL as the convention for that descriptor pair, as the plan requires ([implementation plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:202)). Update `driver.cljc` only where the new connection composition changes its calls or state.\n\nThis leaves the apply envelope solely on the VM’s local FFI call bridge. It does **not** relabel generic DaoStream descriptor or operation envelopes as apply envelopes; those are separate contract data ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:322)).\n\nStatus: COMPLETECompleted-GMT: 2026-09-27 10:47:38 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-27 17:47:38 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**Ruling: Option A — migrate the JVM sketch.** Its useful proof is that a lease judge reclaims a served resource and the holder can observe that loss. The current test reaches that result through `dao.stream.serving` session retirement and WebSocket close, the copy path slice 5 deletes. It also models renewal attribution with a self-asserted, flattened `:ws/attachment` value; the test itself records that this is not the transport’s shape ([test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:740](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:740), [test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:750](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:750), [test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:796](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:796)). The plan retires `dao.stream.serving` in slice 5 ([docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:103](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:103), [docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:188](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:188)).\n\n**Slice 5 replacement:** Keep one active lease composition test, using two in-process ring buffers as a channel, a mirror table, and a holder reflection. Enter one served ring-buffer handle under identity `I`, surface `#{:reader}`, and lease `L`; grant `L` with subject `I`. Wire a separate per-author renewal medium to the judge. After the initial tick, assert the holder’s reflection can resolve and read the served entry. Append a renewal to that medium, step the judge at the existing reading of 7, and assert `:last-observation` is `{:ms 7}`. At reading 18, have the judge’s idempotent reclaim remove `I` from the table and report success. Assert the entry is absent, the grantor writer contains exactly `(lease/lapsed L :silence)`, and the ledger no longer contains `L`. Drive one more reflection request and mirror step; assert the mirror answers `:dao.stream.remote/not-found`, the reflection becomes gone, and a subsequent handle operation returns `:dao.stream/transport-error` with reason `:dao.stream.remote/not-found`. These are the replacement for the old session and WebSocket-close assertions ([test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:803](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:803), [docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:71](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:71), [docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:173](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:173), [docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:258](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:258)).\n\nUse the specified per-author medium rather than carrying over the test’s flattened attachment-key assertion: section 6 makes that medium the holder’s renewal path and requires reclaim to remove the served and renewal entries together ([docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:528](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:528)). Remove the obsolete `serving-fixture`, its private fixture values, and the `dao.stream.serving` require if no other test uses them. Keep the separate carriage sketch’s grant and `:lapsed` assertions; a lapse remains the grantor’s record, while a holder observes reclaim through the reflection ([test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:677](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:677), [docs/design/dao.lease.md:289](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:289), [docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:553](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:553)). No new slice-7 proof row is needed for this single-entry lifetime; slice 7 already covers leased relay pairs ([docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:114](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:114)).\n\nNo files were edited.\n\nStatus: COMPLETE     1\t(ns dao.stream.rpc\n     2\t  \"Transport-neutral, polling RPC state machines for DaoStream v2.\n     3\t\n     4\t   This layer deliberately owns no socket, scheduler, atom, promise, callback,\n     5\t   retry policy, or transport registry.  A composition supplies two explicit\n     6\t   handles (a request writer and a response reader), owns the returned state,\n     7\t   and chooses when to step `request!` and `poll!`.\n     8\t\n     9\t   The request/answer vocabulary below is owned by this namespace, not by\n    10\t   `dao.stream.apply`: a request is `{:dao.stream.rpc/id\n    11\t   :dao.stream.rpc/op :dao.stream.rpc/args}` and an answer is `{:dao.stream.rpc/id\n    12\t   :dao.stream.rpc/ok <value>}` or `{:dao.stream.rpc/id\n    13\t   :dao.stream.rpc/error {:dao.stream.rpc/code :dao.stream.rpc/message}}`.\n    14\t   Ids are self-minted random safe integers, not a sequential counter: callers\n    15\t   sharing one answers stream must not collide on a caller-chosen id\n    16\t   (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md, section 5).\"\n    17\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]))\n    18\t\n    19\t\n    20\t;; =============================================================================\n    21\t;; Public data vocabulary\n    22\t;; =============================================================================\n    23\t\n    24\t(def max-safe-id\n    25\t  \"Largest integer that remains exactly representable by every supported host.\"\n    26\t  9007199254740991)\n    27\t\n    28\t\n    29\t(def event-key :dao.stream.rpc/event)\n    30\t(def response-key :dao.stream.rpc/response)\n    31\t(def diagnostic-key :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic)\n    32\t(def code-key :dao.stream.rpc/code)\n    33\t\n    34\t\n    35\t(def max-id-retries\n    36\t  \"Bound on re-rolling a self-minted id after a collision, before allocation\n    37\t   fails through the existing terminal path.\"\n    38\t  8)\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\t(defn response-event\n    42\t  [response]\n    43\t  {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/response response-key response})\n    44\t\n    45\t\n    46\t(defn diagnostic-event\n    47\t  ([code]\n    48\t   (diagnostic-event code nil))\n    49\t  ([code value]\n    50\t   {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic\n    51\t    diagnostic-key {code-key code\n    52\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/value value}}))\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\t(defn ignore-event\n    56\t  []\n    57\t  {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/ignore})\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\t(defn safe-id?\n    61\t  \"True for an integer which round-trips without precision loss across CLJ,\n    62\t   CLJS, and CLJD.  IDs are routing tokens, not application values.\"\n    63\t  [id]\n    64\t  (and (integer? id) (<= 0 id max-safe-id)))\n    65\t\n    66\t\n    67\t;; =============================================================================\n    68\t;; Request/answer value vocabulary owned by this namespace\n    69\t;; =============================================================================\n    70\t\n    71\t(defn request-value\n    72\t  [id op args]\n    73\t  {:dao.stream.rpc/id id :dao.stream.rpc/op op :dao.stream.rpc/args args})\n    74\t\n    75\t\n    76\t(defn request-value?\n    77\t  [v]\n    78\t  (and (map? v)\n    79\t       (safe-id? (:dao.stream.rpc/id v))\n    80\t       (keyword? (:dao.stream.rpc/op v))\n    81\t       (vector? (:dao.stream.rpc/args v))))\n    82\t\n    83\t\n    84\t(defn request-id [v] (:dao.stream.rpc/id v))\n    85\t(defn request-op [v] (:dao.stream.rpc/op v))\n    86\t(defn request-args [v] (:dao.stream.rpc/args v))\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\t(defn success-answer\n    90\t  [id value]\n    91\t  {:dao.stream.rpc/id id :dao.stream.rpc/ok value})\n    92\t\n    93\t\n    94\t(defn error-answer\n    95\t  [id code message]\n    96\t  {:dao.stream.rpc/id id\n    97\t   :dao.stream.rpc/error {:dao.stream.rpc/code code\n    98\t                          :dao.stream.rpc/message message}})\n    99\t\n   100\t\n   101\t(defn answer?\n   102\t  [v]\n   103\t  (and (map? v)\n   104\t       (safe-id? (:dao.stream.rpc/id v))\n   105\t       (let [ok? (contains? v :dao.stream.rpc/ok)\n   106\t             err? (contains? v :dao.stream.rpc/error)]\n   107\t         (and (or ok? err?) (not (and ok? err?))))))\n   108\t\n   109\t\n   110\t(defn answer-id [answer] (:dao.stream.rpc/id answer))\n   111\t(defn answer-ok [answer] (:dao.stream.rpc/ok answer))\n   112\t(defn answer-error [answer] (:dao.stream.rpc/error answer))\n   113\t\n   114\t\n   115\t(defn answer-ok?\n   116\t  [answer]\n   117\t  (contains? answer :dao.stream.rpc/ok))\n   118\t\n   119\t\n   120\t;; =============================================================================\n   121\t;; Explicit client state\n   122\t;; =============================================================================\n   123\t\n   124\t(defn- random-safe-id\n   125\t  []\n   126\t  #?(:clj (long (* max-safe-id (rand)))\n   127\t     :cljs (Math/floor (* max-safe-id (rand)))\n   128\t     :cljd (.floor ^double (* max-safe-id (rand)))))\n   129\t\n   130\t\n   131\t(defn client-state\n   132\t  \"Create caller-owned RPC client state.\n   133\t\n   134\t   `writer` is the request path. `reader` and `response-cursor` are the\n   135\t   independent response path.  No cursor is fabricated here; a caller\n   136\t   over a dao.stream.remote reflection passes `stream/anchor-newest`\n   137\t   (the request/response service's own mint, docs/design/\n   138\t   dao.stream.remote.md section 5) and `poll!` resolves it, retrying\n   139\t   while the reflection answers a retryable mint -- the same pattern\n   140\t   `dao.jing.content.step/mint-cursor` uses for the content client.\"\n   141\t  ([writer reader response-cursor]\n   142\t   (client-state writer reader response-cursor {}))\n   143\t  ([writer reader response-cursor _opts]\n   144\t   {:writer writer\n   145\t    :reader reader\n   146\t    :cursor response-cursor\n   147\t    :outstanding {}\n   148\t    :unsent nil\n   149\t    :completed []\n   150\t    :diagnostics []\n   151\t    :terminal nil}))\n   152\t\n   153\t\n   154\t(def init-client client-state)\n   155\t\n   156\t\n   157\t(defn- rpc-result\n   158\t  [outcome state & {:as extra}]\n   159\t  (merge {:dao.stream.rpc/outcome outcome\n   160\t          :dao.stream.rpc/state state}\n   161\t         extra))\n   162\t\n   163\t\n   164\t(defn- valid-operation-result\n   165\t  [op result]\n   166\t  (if (stream/valid-outcome? op result)\n   167\t    result\n   168\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   169\t     :dao.stream/error :dao.stream/invalid-operation-result}))\n   170\t\n   171\t\n   172\t(defn- completion\n   173\t  [request & {:keys [response reason]}]\n   174\t  (cond-> {:dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   175\t           :dao.stream.rpc/op (:op request)\n   176\t           :dao.stream.rpc/args (:args request)}\n   177\t    response (assoc :dao.stream.rpc/response response)\n   178\t    reason (assoc :dao.stream.rpc/reason reason)))\n   179\t\n   180\t\n   181\t(defn- append-completion\n   182\t  [state request & {:as fields}]\n   183\t  (update state :completed conj (apply completion request (mapcat identity fields))))\n   184\t\n   185\t\n   186\t(defn- append-diagnostic\n   187\t  [state code value]\n   188\t  (update state :diagnostics conj {code-key code\n   189\t                                   :dao.stream.rpc/value value}))\n   190\t\n   191\t\n   192\t(defn- ids-in-use?\n   193\t  [state id]\n   194\t  (or (= id (get-in state [:unsent :id]))\n   195\t      (contains? (:outstanding state) id)\n   196\t      (boolean (some #(= id (:dao.stream.rpc/id %)) (:completed state)))))\n   197\t\n   198\t\n   199\t;; Defined below with the other completion bookkeeping; allocation failure is\n   200\t;; a terminal transition and owes the same conservative loss as every other.\n   201\t(declare lose-outstanding)\n   202\t\n   203\t\n   204\t(defn- allocation-failure\n   205\t  [state code]\n   206\t  (let [state (-> state\n   207\t                  (lose-outstanding :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error true)\n   208\t                  (append-diagnostic code nil))]\n   209\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error state\n   210\t                :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state)))))\n   211\t\n   212\t\n   213\t(defn- allocate-request\n   214\t  \"Self-mint a random safe id in [0, max-safe-id], re-rolling on a collision\n   215\t   with an id already in use by this state, up to `max-id-retries` times.\n   216\t   This is the request/answer service's own id space (docs/design/\n   217\t   dao.stream.remote.md section 5): callers sharing one answers stream must\n   218\t   not collide on a caller-chosen id, so the id is not a sequential counter.\"\n   219\t  [state op args]\n   220\t  (loop [attempt 0]\n   221\t    (if (>= attempt max-id-retries)\n   222\t      (allocation-failure state :dao.stream.rpc/id-collision)\n   223\t      (let [id (random-safe-id)]\n   224\t        (if (ids-in-use? state id)\n   225\t          (recur (inc attempt))\n   226\t          (let [request {:id id\n   227\t                         :op op\n   228\t                         :args args\n   229\t                         :encoded (request-value id op args)}]\n   230\t            (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/allocated\n   231\t                        (assoc state :unsent request))))))))\n   232\t\n   233\t\n   234\t(defn- accept-unsent\n   235\t  [state request]\n   236\t  (-> state\n   237\t      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   238\t      (assoc-in [:outstanding (:id request)]\n   239\t                {:op (:op request) :args (:args request)})))\n   240\t\n   241\t\n   242\t(defn- attempt-unsent\n   243\t  [state]\n   244\t  (let [request (:unsent state)\n   245\t        append-result (valid-operation-result\n   246\t                        :append!\n   247\t                        (stream/append! (:writer state) (:encoded request)))\n   248\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome append-result)]\n   249\t    (case outcome\n   250\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   251\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/requested\n   252\t                  (accept-unsent state request)\n   253\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   254\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)\n   255\t\n   256\t      :dao.stream/full\n   257\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/pending-request state\n   258\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   259\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)\n   260\t\n   261\t      (:dao.stream/closed :dao.stream/invalid-value :dao.stream/transport-error)\n   262\t      (let [state (-> state\n   263\t                      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   264\t                      (append-completion request :reason outcome))]\n   265\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/request-undeliverable state\n   266\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   267\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason outcome\n   268\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result))\n   269\t\n   270\t      ;; A malformed writer answer becomes transport-error in\n   271\t      ;; valid-operation-result, so this branch is defensive only.\n   272\t      (let [state (-> state\n   273\t                      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   274\t                      (append-completion request :reason :dao.stream/transport-error))]\n   275\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/request-undeliverable state\n   276\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   277\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream/transport-error\n   278\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)))))\n   279\t\n   280\t\n   281\t(defn request!\n   282\t  \"Attempt one request append, returning an explicit next state.\n   283\t\n   284\t   If a previous append was full, this retries the exact already-allocated\n   285\t   envelope and ignores `op`/`args`; it never allocates a replacement id.  The\n   286\t   function makes one append attempt at most and never spins on `:full`.\"\n   287\t  [state op args]\n   288\t  (cond\n   289\t    (:terminal state)\n   290\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/terminal state)\n   291\t\n   292\t    (:unsent state)\n   293\t    (attempt-unsent state)\n   294\t\n   295\t    (not (and (keyword? op) (vector? args)))\n   296\t    (let [state (append-diagnostic state :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request\n   297\t                                   {:op op :args args})]\n   298\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request state\n   299\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   300\t\n   301\t    :else\n   302\t    (let [allocated (allocate-request state op args)]\n   303\t      (if (= :dao.stream.rpc/allocated (:dao.stream.rpc/outcome allocated))\n   304\t        (attempt-unsent (:dao.stream.rpc/state allocated))\n   305\t        allocated))))\n   306\t\n   307\t\n   308\t(defn unsent?\n   309\t  \"True while an allocated request is still owed one accepted append.  A caller\n   310\t   that allocates ids from this state must not submit new work while it holds:\n   311\t   `request!` retries the unsent envelope and ignores the operation it is given.\"\n   312\t  [state]\n   313\t  (some? (:unsent state)))\n   314\t\n   315\t\n   316\t(defn abandon-unsent\n   317\t  \"Give up on an allocated-but-unsent request, appending its completion with\n   318\t   `reason`.\n   319\t\n   320\t   A composition that changes what the writer *is* — an operator disconnect, a\n   321\t   rebind onto a fresh attachment — owns the decision that the retained\n   322\t   envelope no longer belongs to the new binding.  Abandoning it here reports\n   323\t   the loss on the ordinary completion path, so it is neither silently dropped\n   324\t   nor resent in place of the caller's next request.\"\n   325\t  ([state] (abandon-unsent state :dao.stream.rpc/abandoned))\n   326\t  ([state reason]\n   327\t   (if-let [request (:unsent state)]\n   328\t     (-> state\n   329\t         (assoc :unsent nil)\n   330\t         (append-completion request :reason reason))\n   331\t     state)))\n   332\t\n   333\t\n   334\t;; =============================================================================\n   335\t;; Response decoding and correlation\n   336\t;; =============================================================================\n   337\t\n   338\t(defn- normalize-event\n   339\t  [value]\n   340\t  (cond\n   341\t    (nil? value) (ignore-event)\n   342\t    (answer? value) (response-event value)\n   343\t    (and (map? value) (keyword? (get value event-key))) value\n   344\t    :else (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/malformed-response value)))\n   345\t\n   346\t\n   347\t(defn- decode-value\n   348\t  [_state value]\n   349\t  (try\n   350\t    (normalize-event value)\n   351\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   352\t      (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/decode-error value))))\n   353\t\n   354\t\n   355\t(defn- lose-outstanding\n   356\t  [state reason terminal?]\n   357\t  (let [state (reduce (fn [s [id request]]\n   358\t                        (append-completion s (assoc request :id id) :reason reason))\n   359\t                      state\n   360\t                      (:outstanding state))]\n   361\t    (cond-> (assoc state :outstanding {})\n   362\t      terminal? (assoc :terminal reason))))\n   363\t\n   364\t\n   365\t(defn handle-event\n   366\t  \"Apply one decoded RPC event to explicit client state.\n   367\t\n   368\t   `response-event`, `diagnostic-event`, and `ignore-event` are the events a\n   369\t   composition constructs; a bare answer value is also accepted.  This\n   370\t   function has no cursor operation and is useful for testing adapters\n   371\t   independently from a response medium.\"\n   372\t  [state event]\n   373\t  (let [event (normalize-event event)\n   374\t        kind (get event event-key)]\n   375\t    (cond\n   376\t      ;; A terminal binding cannot complete an old request later.  Keep the\n   377\t      ;; event as diagnostic data rather than silently treating it as a reply.\n   378\t      (:terminal state)\n   379\t      (let [state (append-diagnostic state :dao.stream.rpc/event-after-terminal event)]\n   380\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic state\n   381\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   382\t\n   383\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/ignore)\n   384\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/ignored state)\n   385\t\n   386\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic)\n   387\t      (let [diagnostic (get event diagnostic-key)\n   388\t            state (append-diagnostic state (get diagnostic code-key) diagnostic)]\n   389\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic state\n   390\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   391\t\n   392\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/response)\n   393\t      (let [response (get event response-key)]\n   394\t        (if-not (answer? response)\n   395\t          (handle-event state (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/malformed-response response))\n   396\t          (let [id (answer-id response)]\n   397\t            (if-let [request (get (:outstanding state) id)]\n   398\t              (let [state (-> state\n   399\t                              (update :outstanding dissoc id)\n   400\t                              (append-completion (assoc request :id id)\n   401\t                                                 :response response))]\n   402\t                (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/responded state\n   403\t                            :dao.stream.rpc/id id\n   404\t                            :dao.stream.rpc/response response))\n   405\t              (handle-event state\n   406\t                            (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/unsolicited-response response))))))\n   407\t\n   408\t      :else\n   409\t      (handle-event state (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/unhandled-event event)))))\n   410\t\n   411\t\n   412\t(defn- transport-error-reason\n   413\t  \"Translate a reflection's `:dao.stream/transport-error` into the client\n   414\t   terminal vocabulary, inspecting `:dao.stream.remote/reason` when the\n   415\t   reflection supplied one (dao.stream.remote.cljc reasons a reflection's\n   416\t   read failure this way).  A reason this translation does not recognize\n   417\t   still becomes the generic transport-error terminal, never left raw.\"\n   418\t  [read-result]\n   419\t  (case (:dao.stream.remote/reason read-result)\n   420\t    :dao.stream.remote/not-found :dao.stream.apply/not-found\n   421\t    :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone :dao.stream.apply/detached\n   422\t    :dao.stream.apply/transport-error))\n   423\t\n   424\t\n   425\t(defn- mint-cursor\n   426\t  \"Resolve an anchor-keyword cursor through the reader: a reflection's\n   427\t   mint is answered across the channel, so a retryable answer leaves the\n   428\t   anchor in place for a later poll (dao.jing.content.step/mint-cursor is\n   429\t   the precedent this mirrors).  Returns `[cursor settled?]`.\"\n   430\t  [reader anchor]\n   431\t  (let [r (stream/cursor reader anchor)]\n   432\t    (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n   433\t      [(:dao.stream/cursor r) true]\n   434\t      [anchor false])))\n   435\t\n   436\t\n   437\t(defn- poll-read\n   438\t  [state]\n   439\t  (let [read-result (valid-operation-result :next (stream/next (:reader state) (:cursor state)))\n   440\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome read-result)]\n   441\t    (case outcome\n   442\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   443\t      ;; Advance before decoding. A malformed or unsolicited element is thereby\n   444\t      ;; consumed exactly once and cannot poison polling.\n   445\t      (handle-event (assoc state :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result))\n   446\t                    (decode-value state (:dao.stream/value read-result)))\n   447\t\n   448\t      :dao.stream/blocked\n   449\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result)\n   450\t\n   451\t      :dao.stream/gap\n   452\t      (let [state (-> state\n   453\t                      (assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result))\n   454\t                      (lose-outstanding :dao.stream/gap false))]\n   455\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   456\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream/gap\n   457\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result))\n   458\t\n   459\t      :dao.stream/end\n   460\t      (let [state (lose-outstanding state :dao.stream.apply/ended true)]\n   461\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   462\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream.apply/ended\n   463\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result))\n   464\t\n   465\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n   466\t      (let [reason (transport-error-reason read-result)\n   467\t            state (lose-outstanding state reason true)]\n   468\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   469\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason reason\n   470\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result))\n   471\t\n   472\t      (let [state (lose-outstanding state :dao.stream.apply/transport-error true)]\n   473\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   474\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream.apply/transport-error\n   475\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result)))))\n   476\t\n   477\t\n   478\t(defn poll!\n   479\t  \"Read at most `budget` response-medium elements.\n   480\t\n   481\t   A `:dao.stream/blocked` result yields immediately; this function never loops\n   482\t   waiting for a response.  Each successful read installs the exact successor\n   483\t   cursor returned by that handle before decoding the element.\"\n   484\t  ([state] (poll! state 1))\n   485\t  ([state budget]\n   486\t   (cond\n   487\t     (:terminal state) (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/terminal state)\n   488\t     (not (and (integer? budget) (pos? budget)))\n   489\t     (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state)\n   490\t     :else\n   491\t     (loop [remaining budget\n   492\t            state state\n   493\t            last-result nil]\n   494\t       (cond\n   495\t         (zero? remaining) (or last-result (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state))\n   496\t\n   497\t         ;; The response cursor is an unresolved anchor: settling it is not\n   498\t         ;; a read and does not spend the budget, exactly as\n   499\t         ;; dao.jing.content.step/poll's own anchor branch does not.\n   500\t         (contains? stream/standard-anchors (:cursor state))\n   501\t         (let [[cursor settled?] (mint-cursor (:reader state) (:cursor state))]\n   502\t           (if settled?\n   503\t             (recur remaining (assoc state :cursor cursor) last-result)\n   504\t             (or last-result (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state))))\n   505\t\n   506\t         :else\n   507\t         (let [result (poll-read state)\n   508\t               next-state (:dao.stream.rpc/state result)\n   509\t               outcome (:dao.stream.rpc/outcome result)]\n   510\t           (if (or (:terminal next-state)\n   511\t                   (= outcome :dao.stream.rpc/idle))\n   512\t             result\n   513\t             (recur (dec remaining) next-state result))))))))\n   514\t\n   515\t\n   516\t(defn take-completed\n   517\t  \"Return `[completions next-state]`, clearing the unpublished completion\n   518\t   outbox exactly once.  The caller owns publication and must call this only\n   519\t   from its single state-owning driver step.\"\n   520\t  [state]\n   521\t  [(:completed state) (assoc state :completed [])])\n   522\t\n   523\t\n   524\t(defn take-diagnostics\n   525\t  \"Return `[diagnostics next-state]`, clearing the unpublished diagnostic outbox\n   526\t   exactly once.  Diagnostics are bounded by publication exactly as completions\n   527\t   are: a noisy shared response medium can produce one per read, so a caller\n   528\t   that never publishes would grow this vector for the session's lifetime.\"\n   529\t  [state]\n   530\t  [(:diagnostics state) (assoc state :diagnostics [])])\n   531\t\n   532\t\n   533\t(defn rebind\n   534\t  \"Replace a detached client's writer (and optionally reader) without creating\n   535\t   a cursor or replacing the random id allocator. Only `/detached` is\n   536\t   reconnectable; other terminal reasons remain terminal.\"\n   537\t  ([state writer]\n   538\t   (if (= :dao.stream.apply/detached (:terminal state))\n   539\t     (assoc state :writer writer :terminal nil)\n   540\t     state))\n   541\t  ([state writer reader]\n   542\t   (if (= :dao.stream.apply/detached (:terminal state))\n   543\t     (assoc state :writer writer :reader reader :terminal nil)\n   544\t     state)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
